# The `between` references that land on a layer this corpus holds. One row per resolved reference,
# carrying the filing the notation resolves to.
#
# The positive set, named
#   Its complement is the interesting one. Name the union and take its complement, because in a
#   model about typed absence the positive set is always the meaningful one.
#   `eliminations/references.sqlc` minus this is *the references that point outside what is here*,
#   which the schema calls ordinary rather than wrong.
#
# Resolution is a reader's assertion one step removed
#   Exactly as it is for a part: `filing_identity` is what a document says about itself, and without
#   it a cross-document reference cannot be resolved from the corpus even in principle.
#
# `pm.layer` directly, not `layers/every_layer.sqlc`
#   The last join is a membership test: does the layer this reference names exist.
#   `layers/every_layer.sqlc` is the layer dimension, reserved for denominators. Composed here it
#   would drag the entire dimension into the transitive closure of most of the checker, and any
#   reach-containment law over a rule is vacuous the moment the dimension is inside its closure,
#   because the dimension reaches everything by construction. `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc` states
#   the rule and enforces it over the compose DAG.
-- eliminations/references.sqlc joined through pm.filing_identity to pm.layer.
SELECT b.composition, b.composed_layer, b.quantity, b.seq,
       b.party, b.notation, fi.filing AS resolved_filing, b.layer, b.regime
FROM      (
    :compose(eliminations/references.sqlc)
) b
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/notations.sqlc)
) fi ON fi.notation = b.notation
JOIN pm.layer l  ON l.filing = fi.filing AND l.layer = b.layer
